#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135063 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to construct a simple linear regression model given some bivariate data $(x,y).$ We can generalize this technique to fit data to an arbitrary polynomial. We call this process **polynomial regression**.\n\nThe following table gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units:\n\n| Goods $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $16$ | $11$ | $15$ | $18$ |\n\nSuppose we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/93/93fe1784b89ce4c892c84a03a0dc80909f16f46ac463c5cc30b40f869a7802bf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+1{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=16 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n![](images/72/729e20ddab6e49b109ebccbb049b1abb9ca2fc11003a9e7f4828bff305a283b4.svg)\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the curve that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}22.5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the quadratic curve of best fit (regression curve) is \n\n$$\ny = 22.5 - 9x + 2x^2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a9d76d67-952b-5a5c-8891-757974db3a7f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3654 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135064 :tutorial/content "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\n  \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2}=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2}=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**." :tutorial/id #uuid "ac48d158-e2b0-57f9-b8ba-6fe587ca807a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3655 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135142 :tutorial/content "Given a dataset of paired numerical observations $(x,y)$ and the corresponding linear regression model $\\widehat{y} =f(x),$ the **residual** for a particular observation $(x, y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-f(x) \\\\  & =y-\\hat{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe subtract the *estimated* value predicted by the model from the actual *observed* value. The residuals for the data set we considered earlier are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/515aaaeef2f85bb131564b92bdaef9ef514d05c8028ae85382d231565d490665.png)\n\nIn the past, you may have seen that  calculating a regression line typically means finding a line where the sum of the squared residuals \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2\n$$\n\nis as small as possible. \n\nUsing matrix notation, the sum of the squared residuals can be viewed as \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2 = \\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the condition that the sum of the squared residuals is minimized is equivalent to minimizing $\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2.$ This, in turn, is equivalent to minimizing $\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|.$ \n\nTherefore, the problem of minimizing the squared residuals is equivalent to solving the least squares problem for $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eeb5c2f5-02c9-5579-97b5-0393df974a32" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6149 :tutorial/title "Least-Squares vs. Minimizing the Sum of the Squared Residuals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188207 :question/id #uuid "0eddf8b0-bdd9-55db-9388-d39bd91c24c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7251" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^4 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^4$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^4.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\textrm{rank}(T)=3$\n\nII. $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T})=3$\n\nIII. $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^4$ into $\\mathbb{R}^4,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) ![](images/5d/5d330995eaf0f71f9006f897a75e9e892caecb94eb8c8b48d7ba7f116633b6e3.svg) \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) + \\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ and the set $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\textrm{rank}(T)=\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =4-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 4$ (or since $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq 0$), the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef4c3240-efcc-5031-be97-b18f24fa624c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477283} {:answer/id #uuid "80eef841-b3bb-539f-a20d-b7af5fdc129f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477283} :answer-field/id #uuid "97b22468-5364-5814-ab56-00d3827125a4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477611} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ddb04cf-498a-5aa6-bd12-7e6b3799bde4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477285} {:answer/id #uuid "6462c36b-1577-5e5f-980f-afbd1f21ace1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477286} :answer-field/id #uuid "f417e3d1-8fa9-576f-97f4-84f0c7ed2417" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477612} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7971526-d720-5bf2-916b-1c3268cfc3a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477287} {:answer/id #uuid "ca68341f-f266-5714-a2d3-03c3de4d3096" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477287} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf2f78e9-40f7-51c7-9823-236bb1c047ce" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477613}] :question/id #uuid "e86ac40c-9f96-593f-88c6-aef65bbc3b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-38136" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=1$\n- **II.** $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$\n- **III.** $T$ is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192613 :question/id #uuid "ad59f3c2-8ca8-5d05-b626-501682800f01" :question/math-academy-id "e-21884" :question/problem "A financial analyst measured $5$ features, $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_5,$ for each company among a group of companies under study. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular company.\n\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1=7, \\quad \\sigma_2=5, \\quad \\sigma_3=3, \\quad \\sigma_4=2, \\quad \\sigma_5=1.\n$$\n\nThe analyst then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y = X V_2,$ where $V_2$ is the $5 \\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY = XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_5$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_5$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values $\\sigma_1^2, \\sigma_2^2, \\ldots, \\sigma_5^2$ represent the variances in the respective features $y_1,y_2,\\ldots,y_5.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2}+(5)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2} \\\\  & =88\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the analyst used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2}+(5)^{2} \\\\  & =74.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{74}{88} \\\\  & ≈0.841 \\\\  & =84.1\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac7d70fa-bf88-5d8a-b589-211fc1508e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "86.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191824} {:answer/id #uuid "f22fa831-0ffa-5b44-8d4d-6444a734e999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191825} {:answer/id #uuid "229c730c-c45a-5588-82ae-49b26c273ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191826} {:answer/id #uuid "41964a93-85b9-57dd-b859-9b726749f309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191827} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4abaff-9204-5ea0-a799-46b24af32d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191826} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c783572-57af-5f59-b3ff-cca2d8bf3bb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191823}] :question/id #uuid "edd41528-ab75-576f-8bc0-8fe5a9ab1f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-343713" :question/problem "A scientist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each specimen among a group of animals in danger of extinction. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular specimen.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=7,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.6,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.4.\n$$\n\nThe scientist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $5\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.6)^{2}+(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =54.52\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the scientist used only the first principal component, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(7)^{2} \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{49}{54.52} \\\\  & ≈0.899 \\\\  & =89.9\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "929589f9-2c1b-52ff-83d3-fa8d2f2ee07c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "91.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191830} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c2a84c-85c4-59d7-962e-1ed8ea8a8e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191831} {:answer/id #uuid "0dac9873-d06e-599b-b424-744747db4a4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191832} {:answer/id #uuid "43f7b588-fc22-5ed8-8e11-88e773839d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "97.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191833} {:answer/id #uuid "61cec5a4-6e98-53d4-b55b-1952616525f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "79.0%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191832} :answer-field/id #uuid "705a4ca2-8121-50c8-969c-f904844cb959" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191829}] :question/id #uuid "cd0aa760-2231-5a4e-8f82-cb5ce0612345" :question/math-academy-id "q-345593" :question/problem "A scientist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each athlete among a group of athletes. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular athlete.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=5,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.5,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.1.\n$$\n\nThe scientist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{2},$ where ${V}_{2}$ is the $5\\times 2$ matrix consisting of the first two principal components of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the matrix of principal components. Then, applying the change of variables\n\n$$\nY=XV\n$$\n\nwe can convert our observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ of (linearly) transformed observations such that\n\n- the covariance matrix of $Y$ is diagonal (meaning that the new features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are uncorrelated),\n\n- the features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}$ are arranged in order of decreasing variance, and\n\n- the squares of the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2},…,{\\sigma }_{5}^{2}$ represent the variances in the respective features ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{5}.$\n\nNow, the total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(0.5)^{2}+(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =30.26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the scientist used only the first two principal components, the remaining variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Remaining} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)^{2}+(2)^{2} \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Remaining}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{29}{30.26} \\\\  & ≈0.958 \\\\  & =95.8\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193808 :tutorial/content "A nutritionist measured $3$ features among a group of clients:\n\n- body mass index ($x_1$)\n\n- height ($x_2$)\n\n- weight ($x_3$)\n\nThe collected data was stored in the mean-centered observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular client. The nutritionist finds that the singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1=12, \\qquad \\sigma_2=5, \\qquad \\sigma_3=4.\n$$\n\nThe nutritionist then decides to reduce the dimensionality of the data set. To do this, they convert the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation \n\n$$\nY = X V_1,\n$$\n\nwhere $V_1$ is the $3 \\times 1$ matrix containing the first principal component of the data only. As a result, $Y$ is an $n \\times 1$ matrix that gives a single feature associated with each client.\n\nWhat percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$\n\nThe total variance over all features is proportional to the sum of the squared singular values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(12)^{2}+(5)^{2}+(4)^{2} \\\\  & =185.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the nutritionist used only the first principal component, the preserved variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Preserved} & ∝{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(12)^{2} \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our required percentage can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{Preserved}}{\\text{Total}} & =\\frac{144}{185} \\\\  & ≈0.778 \\\\  & =77.8\\%.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- The nutritionist reduced the dimensionality of the data from $3$ (initially, each client had three features) to just $1$ (only one feature characterizes each client after the transformation).\n\n- In doing so, $77.8\\%$ of the variance is preserved in the transformed data." :tutorial/id #uuid "bde3e2b4-d644-5955-bbda-5e4e79c40df2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14788 :tutorial/title "Computing the Remaining Variance After Dimensionality Reduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1e3e0f1-7153-5d1e-b2fd-e5ed9412effc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the direction in feature space" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203524} {:answer/id #uuid "e5045f4c-fb9e-59fe-b2bc-e6d7604629f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the datapoint" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the direction in feature space" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203524} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ea042d6-6a31-56d0-a56c-27a81fc3